I tried some tests but I was not able to repro the issue. I think the problem is that I can't find the way to randomly delay the requests. Using the following command for testing on docker
{code:java}
lein run test --nodes-file /root/nodes --username root  -s --no-ww-deps --txn --concurrency 3n --nemesis pause,kill,partition --intra-txn-delay 50 --db-targets one,majority –partition-targets one,majority,majorities-ring --nemesis-interval 10 --abort-p 0.15 --crash-clients --time-limit 1200 --test-count 10  --db kafka_kraft_3_8 {code}
Any suggestions?

Yes, this issue exists across all Java versions. Apologies for referencing an outdated document


https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/api/java.base/java/lang/Object.html#wait()

OpenJDK has recommended the following solution.


{code:java}
API Note:
The recommended approach to waiting is to check the condition being awaited in a while loop around the call to wait, as shown in the example below. Among other things, this approach avoids problems that can be caused by spurious wakeups.

     synchronized (obj) {
         while (<condition does not hold> and <timeout not exceeded>) {
             long timeoutMillis = ... ; // recompute timeout values
             int nanos = ... ;
             obj.wait(timeoutMillis, nanos);
         }
         ... // Perform action appropriate to condition or timeout
     }
 

{code}

{quote}
 I had a look and my understanding is that the consumers used by Sources/Sinks are configured correctly
{quote}

They were not correctly configured in the end-to-end (e2e) tests because session.timeout.ms was present in the worker configuration. That caused errors when running the sink connector with consumer.override.group.protocol=consumer. KAFKA-18132 has already fixed this issue.

{quote}
However, the ones used internally by Connect (e.g. to store offsets) are not configured to use the new consumer. Do you confirm my understanding?
{quote}

Yes, you're right. Perhaps we should run Connect store by new consumer to uncover more potential issues.

